                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Mistakes When Choosing an Asphalt Sealer
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Most disappointment with driveway sealer comes from a mismatch between the product and the driveway, decided in the aisle before any work started.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Concrete Sealer for an Asphalt Driveway</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        The two are completely different chemistries. Concrete sealers are designed to penetrate or film over a mineral surface; asphalt sealers are emulsions designed to bond with bitumen. Use one on the other and it either beads up and never bonds or fails within months. Check the label for the surface, not just the word "driveway".
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by Price per Pail Alone</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        A cheap sealer is usually cheap because it carries more water and fewer solids, which means less protection per coat and a shorter interval before you do the job again. Compare coverage per gallon and expected life, not shelf price — the labour is the same either way.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming More Coats Means More Years</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Beyond two thin coats, additional sealer adds thickness without adding protection, and thick sealer is brittle sealer. Layers built up over successive years are the usual cause of the cracked, flaking surface people mistake for failing asphalt.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking Sand or Additive Content</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Filled sealers with sand or fine aggregate give better traction and fill surface texture, while unfilled ones give a smoother, darker finish that shows every imperfection. Neither is wrong, but buying the wrong one for a sloped or shaded drive means either a slippery surface or a finish you did not want.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
